Lorentz force is the force exerted on a charged particle by electric and magnetic fields. 
It determines how charged particles move in electromagnitic environments and underlies many physical phenomena. 

The Lorentz force has two components: 
	
	The electic force acts in the direction of the electiric field for positive charges and opposite to it for negative charges, tending to accelerat the particle in a straight line. 
	
	The magnetic force is perpendicular to both the particle's velocity and the magnetic field, and it causes the particle to move along a curved trajectory, often circular or helical in form, depending on the directions of the fields. 
